#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""teddyos-setup — the first-boot journey, ported to Linux.

`kernel/src/setup.rs` describes the shape and it is kept: "One decision per
screen, centred on a white page, with a single primary action and a quiet way
back." Its step order was

    Welcome -> Experience -> Region -> Capabilities -> Skills -> Done

and this is that, minus Region.

Region is dropped deliberately rather than stubbed. On the kernel it set the
clock and the locale because nothing else could; on Linux the installer and
GNOME already ask, and a third screen asking a third time — which then has to
either fight them or quietly do nothing — is worse than not asking. A step that
collects an answer it cannot act on is the same defect as a permission screen
that describes a restriction nothing enforces.

The two steps that carry "people have different needs":

  Experience   how much the machine explains itself. Guided or Advanced.
               Per core/src/level.rs this changes COPY ONLY — "privacy-first
               default grants stay the same at every level". A level that
               quietly granted more would trade the user's privacy for their
               convenience without telling them.

  Skills       which playbooks load. Two people with identical grants still
               want different ones; a trader has no use for what a writer
               lives in. Skills are markdown, and none of them can grant
               itself a capability — a skill runs under what the previous
               screen granted or it does not run.
"""

